# WATER READY — REAL OSM COAST + CURLING SURF

## API

```js
import { buildOcean, testOcean } from './water/ocean.js';

const ocean = buildOcean(engine, field);
// ocean: { group, update(t, dt), stats, shore }
// group includes the Phillips sea, instanced curling ribbons and spray points.
// Main program adds group once and calls update; no second shore texture is created.

testOcean(engine, field);
// => { position, target, fov: 56 }, using field.coastX(-174) when field is supplied.

engine.registry.surfPeriod;
// Seeded mean breaker period in seconds for audio reference.
```

`shore` is the exact object returned by `field.createShoreData(512)`. `stats.breakers` contains the surf sub-budget. Existing callers that only read `{group,update,stats}` remain compatible. Quality is sampled at build time.

## Implementation

- `ocean.js` calls `field.createShoreData(512)` exactly once. The old sine coastline was removed. Both ocean vertex/fragment shaders and surf shaders sample `coastLut` with `uv=(z-minZ)/(maxZ-minZ), 0.5`; `coastRange=(-5000,5000)` and the shore bounds are `x=-400..1300,z=-1400..1400`.
- OSM metadata was inspected without dumping the JSON: coastline way `9410979`, `natural=coastline`, `source=PGS`; attribution `© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL 1.0`. Origin `21.927806,120.849472`, source bbox `120.842,21.918,120.858,21.938`.
- Existing directional Phillips spectrum, deep-water dispersion, two inverse FFT transforms, 24 Hz cap, multiscale ripples, Beer–Lambert shallow transparency, analytic sky reflection, GGX glitter and animated lace foam are retained.
- `breakers.js` adds short geometric crests along the LUT coast. Each instance has seeded length 8–26 m, active life 3.2–5.0 s, an additional 2.5–7.0 s gap, travel from 70–105 m to 44–56 m east of OSM coastX, height 0.45–1.15 m and width 0.9–1.8 m. Local LUT derivatives orient the shore normal; travel is mainly westward.
- The ribbon has an 8×6 segment grid and interpolates from a rising wave profile to an overhanging curl profile before decaying. This is actual vertex geometry, not a white texture. The checked normalized curl profile reaches x=-0.63884 with an overhang above y=0.5.
- High quality has 56 ribbons and 112 soft spray points; low has 40 and 80. Spray has independent emission phase, 0.55–1.05 s dissipation, shore/tangent/up velocities, wind drift and `g=9.81` ballistic fall. Point size follows the renderer drawing-buffer height.
- Surf samples the shared FFT height texture for sea alignment and the shared 512 shore texture for water/land masking. Fragment-level masks reject high land/path coverage. Ocean, ribbon and spray use premultiplied NormalBlending, `depthWrite=false`, `depthTest=true`, and renderOrder 1/2/3. The double-sided ribbon uses `forceSinglePass=true`.
- Surf animation is evaluated on the GPU from `env.time`; `update()` only refreshes the drawing-buffer height and does not iterate over crests or particles. No external asset, loader, private clock or random source was added.

